# Intent: The grid games run on the unit: Four in a Row (id connect4, against the unit, tap a column) and Memory Match (id match, 16 cards, fewest turns wins). The board is drawn by one LVGL object with a draw callback (cells are drawn with rectangles, so the internal cost is one object, not one per cell), the 30 ms game tick running the unit's sliced search one slice a tick, cues through the sound policy, the best score in the unit's NVS. self.game.start and self.game.scores know both ids, self.game.list shows them with kind builtin (and marks the data packs builtin or installed), the games page lists them. Nothing exists while no grid game runs; a game never moves the head; full quiet, the lock and updates refuse or end it as for every game.
# Placement: unit
# Upstream: firmware/main/hal (ours)
# Check after a flash: self.game.list shows connect4 and match with kind builtin; self.game.start connect4 draws the board, a tap in a column drops an amber disc and the unit answers with a red one within a second; self.game.start match shows 16 face-down cards, two taps turn two cards and a mismatch turns back after about a second; a swipe down from the grabber ends the game; full quiet refuses it.
# Kconfig: CONFIG_MAIPAI_GAMES (existing)
# Memory cost: nothing while idle (about 8 KB of flash for the code, measured: the plain image grew 7,840 bytes with the first version). A running game holds the Grid struct (about 0.3 KB) in PSRAM and 2 LVGL objects (the board and the game layer root, plus the drag bar the other games have); the internal cost is estimated at 600 bytes and is measured by two self.bench.heap_info calls (before the start, during the game), still to run on the unit.

